From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ios-blink-sideload <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 14:27:39 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] iOS sideload: follow rotations

Turning the phone left the page wrong in three ways.

The page drew frames the size of the screen
(GetCompositorViewportPixelSize()), but the browser compositor gave it a
new surface only when the view's size changed. A rotation changed the
screen's size without a new surface: the simulator hit a DCHECK in
AsyncLayerTreeFrameSink (a frame 1125 pixels high in a surface whose
frames had been 2436), and a phone showed the page cropped next to an
area the page never drew (red here, gray on the phone). Use the size the
browser compositor shows the page at, as the Mac does: each change of it
comes with a new surface.

The screen info was one rotation behind. display::Screen updated in a
task posted when the device's orientation changed, which ran before the
interface rotated and the scene had its new size: the page was told the
previous size, and "portrait-secondary" in landscape, and a tab opened
after turning back to portrait was drawn at the landscape height. Follow
the window scene's interface orientation, with the bounds that go with
it, and observe the scene's effectiveGeometry, Apple's recommended way
to follow rotations. Views now also update their screen info when the
display changes, not only when they lay out.

The page's view sat in the scroll view at the page's scroll offset, but
UIKit or Chrome moves the scroll view's contentOffset during a rotation
(to -42 here), so the page was drawn 42 points below the toolbar, over
the scroll view's gray background. Place the view at the scroll view's
contentOffset, and follow it when it changes.

Areas without a frame of the page showed upstream's debugging color, red.
Show the page's background color instead, as the Mac does.

Status: verified at Chromium 3313f827 in the iOS 27 simulator, rotating
with UIKit's own calls (UIDevice's orientation, then the scene's
requestGeometryUpdateWithPreferences:): a test page reporting its sizes
and screen.orientation got 744x313 with a 812x375 landscape-primary
screen turned left, landscape-secondary turned right and 375x631 with a
375x812 portrait-primary screen back in portrait, each with a change
event; it was drawn whole, right below the toolbar, in landscape and
after turning back (before: cropped next to red in landscape, 42 points
lower with a gray band in portrait, and the DCHECK). chatgpt.com, opened
after a rotation and then rotated both ways, was drawn whole (before: its
lower part red). Not checked on a device.

